<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    Decompiler MODE presets (kuna_decomp::modes, Architecture::apply_mode): the
    `mode reliable | aggressive` console command applies a named batch of option
    overrides through the SAME binary to decompile path a real `kuna decompile`
    / `decompile-all` uses.  This is the end to end proof that a mode reaches the
    engine (standing requirement: every output changing feature ships a
    binary to decompile testcase; here the "flag" is the whole preset).

    `reliable` == the shipped defaults (an empty override list, a no op alias);
    `aggressive` turns ON every recovery/analysis pass, INCLUDING `returndup`
    (angr ReturnDuplicatorHigh, p8_structure/kuna_returndup.rs).  `returndup` is
    a shipped default since DIV-54, but `aggressive` still names it, so the preset
    OWNS the option: an explicit `option returndup off` earlier in the script is
    re-enabled by `mode aggressive` and not by `mode reliable`.  That makes
    `returndup`s emit only warning ("// return-dupe N") the clean discriminator
    between the two modes even after the default flip.

    The reproducer is the sibling ghangr-returndup.xml witness: an if/else if guard
    chain over `a0` whose three paths each `mov eax, K; jmp .Lend` into a single
    shared bare `ret` epilogue (3 in edges, the return value a MULTIEQUAL of
    {1,2,3}).  returndup duplicates that shared epilogue into 2 of its 3
    predecessors, logging the warning once.

    Pass 1 (option returndup off, then mode reliable): the empty reliable override
    list leaves the explicit off in place, so the warning never appears.
    Pass 2 (mode aggressive): the preset re-enables returndup, so the warning
    appears exactly once.

    The default on siblings earlyreturn (DIV-23) and switchreturn (DIV-25) are on
    in BOTH passes (they are shipped defaults, part of `reliable`), so they do not
    affect the returndup only warning count; they are left at their defaults here
    precisely to show that `aggressive` restores returndup ON TOP of an otherwise
    default set the user has locally overridden.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">
85ff7507b801000000eb1183ff017507b802000000eb05b803000000c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="f"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <!-- Pass 1: returndup explicitly OFF, then reliable (an empty override list, so
       the off survives).  DIV-54 made returndup a shipped default, so the explicit
       off is what the mode has to overturn in pass 2. -->
  <com>option returndup off</com>
  <com>mode reliable</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <!-- Pass 2: aggressive turns returndup (and the rest of the off by default
       passes) ON.  The only observable that is returndup exclusive is its warning. -->
  <com>mode aggressive</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<!-- (DIV-39 warnstyle: warnings render as terse // slugs at end of line; asserts re-worded.) -->
<!-- Assertion name kept verbatim so the stages baseline needs no re-record: it is still
     exactly what the two passes show, now against an explicit pass-1 `returndup off`. -->
<stringmatch name="mode aggressive enables returndup (warning appears once); mode reliable does not" min="1" max="1">return-dupe x2</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
